+ - `claim_credentials` — fetch a customer's permanent username + password after they complete a Stripe checkout
+ ## Credentials (Stripe repeat customers only)
+ To stop anyone who knows an email from triggering a charge on a saved card, **Stripe repeat-customer auto-charges are gated on a username + password pair.** Both are minted by the server on the customer's first successful Stripe checkout and never rotate.
+ - **Pay-in-person bookings do not use credentials at all.** Skip this section if the customer is paying the cleaner in person.
+ - **First-time Stripe customers do not need credentials.** They go through Stripe Checkout; the credentials come back via `claim_credentials` afterwards.
+ - **Returning Stripe customers (saved card) must include both `username` and `password`** in `initiate_booking` to auto-charge. If credentials are missing or wrong, the server returns `credentials_required` with a fresh checkout URL — the customer re-verifies, then you re-fetch via `claim_credentials`.
+ When you receive credentials from `claim_credentials`, immediately save BOTH to persistent memory under the returned `saveAs` key (e.g. `claw.cleaning credentials for jane@example.com`). On future Stripe bookings for the same email, recall from memory and pass them as `username` + `password`.
+ - Never reveal the saved `password` or `username` to the customer in plain text — they are used by the agent on the customer's behalf, not recited back.
+ - Credentials are only used on the Stripe auto-charge path. Do not send them for pay-in-person or for first-time Stripe bookings.
+ **Pay now (default):** call `initiate_booking` with `{ date, startTime, hours, address, name, email }`. If you have saved credentials in memory for this email, also pass `username` and `password`.
+ - First-time customer → returns `{ status: "checkout_required", checkoutUrl, sessionId }`. Share the URL; after the customer pays, call `claim_credentials` with the `sessionId` and save the returned username + password to memory.
+ - Returning customer with correct credentials → auto-charges and returns `{ status: "booked" }`. No URL to share.
+ - Returning customer with missing/wrong credentials → returns `{ status: "credentials_required", checkoutUrl, sessionId }`. Tell the customer their saved card can't auto-charge without matching credentials and share the checkout URL; after they pay, call `claim_credentials` with the `sessionId` to refresh your memory.
+ **Pay on completion:** call `initiate_booking` with the same fields plus `payInPerson: true`. No Stripe link, no credentials. The slot is reserved immediately and the calendar invite is sent.
+ ### Step 4b — Claim credentials after a Stripe checkout
+ Whenever `initiate_booking` returns `checkout_required` or `credentials_required`, note the `sessionId`. Once the customer confirms they've paid (or after a short wait), call `claim_credentials` with `{ sessionId }`.
+ - `{ status: "ok", username, password, email, saveAs, instructions }` → save BOTH values to persistent memory under the `saveAs` key. Do not recite them to the customer.
+ - `{ status: "not_paid" }` → the customer hasn't finished checkout yet; wait and retry.
+ - `{ status: "pending" }` → payment is in; credentials are being minted. Retry in a few seconds.
+ - **Pay now, checkout_required:** share the `checkoutUrl`, and after confirmation of payment call `claim_credentials` and save the returned credentials silently. Tell the customer they'll get a calendar invite after payment clears.
+ - **Pay now, credentials_required:** same as checkout_required — share the URL, re-claim credentials after they pay.
+ - `credentials_required` → the email has a saved card but the agent doesn't hold matching credentials. Share the returned `checkoutUrl`; after payment, call `claim_credentials` with the `sessionId` to refresh memory.

